        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p><strong>Pay per click (PPC) advertising</strong> means you pay only when someone clicks your ad. On <strong>Google Ads</strong>, your ad competes in an auction each time someone searches, and <strong>Ad Rank</strong> (your bid combined with ad and landing page quality) decides whether you show and where. To start: set up conversion tracking, pick a few high-intent keywords, write relevant ads, send clicks to a focused landing page, set a daily budget you can afford, and review results weekly.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026 · Written for US small businesses</p>

        <p>For many small businesses, <strong>pay per click advertising</strong> is the fastest way to appear in front of people who are ready to buy. It is also one of the easiest ways to waste money if you launch without a plan. This guide explains how PPC works, how <strong>Google Ads</strong> sets what you pay, how to budget, how to launch your first campaign, and how nonprofits can use Google Ad Grants.</p>
        <p>If you are still choosing between platforms, see our comparison of <a href="paid-advertising-platforms-compared.html">paid advertising platforms</a>. If you are weighing PPC against organic search, read the <a href="importance-of-seo-in-2026.html">importance of SEO in 2026</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="what-is-ppc">What is pay per click advertising?</h2>
        <p>PPC is a pricing model, not a single platform. You create an ad, choose who should see it, and pay the platform each time someone clicks. It differs from paying for impressions (how often the ad is shown) because you are billed for an action that sends a real visitor to your site. The <strong>Google Ads platform</strong> is the largest PPC platform for search, but Microsoft Advertising, Meta and LinkedIn also offer click-based buying.</p>
        <p>PPC works best when three things line up: people are searching for what you sell, your ad matches what they want, and your landing page makes it easy to take the next step.</p>

        <h2 id="how-it-works">How Google Ads works: the auction and Ad Rank</h2>
        <p>Each time someone searches, Google runs an auction among eligible advertisers. It does not simply favor the highest bidder. According to Google, <strong>Ad Rank</strong> is a set of values used to decide whether your ads are eligible to show and where they appear. The factors include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your bid:</strong> the maximum amount you are willing to pay for a click. Your actual cost is often lower.</li>
          <li><strong>Ad and landing page quality:</strong> how useful and relevant your ad and the page behind it are, summarized in Quality Score.</li>
          <li><strong>Ad Rank thresholds:</strong> minimum quality levels an ad must meet to show.</li>
          <li><strong>Competitiveness of the auction:</strong> ads with similar Ad Ranks have similar chances, while a larger gap favors the higher-ranked ad.</li>
          <li><strong>Search context:</strong> the search terms, location, device and time of search.</li>
          <li><strong>Expected impact of assets and formats:</strong> such as sitelinks and other extensions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The practical result is that quality can beat budget. Google notes that higher quality ads can often lead to lower cost per click. A well-matched ad and landing page can win a better position at a lower price than a careless competitor with a bigger bid.</p>
        <div class="article-pull">"In PPC, relevance is a discount. The better your ad and page match the search, the less you tend to pay."</div>

        <h2 id="costs">What does PPC cost?</h2>
        <p>There is no single price. Cost per click depends on your industry, the keyword, your location and how many advertisers want the same searches. Legal, insurance, finance and software keywords tend to be expensive, while niche local services can be much cheaper. What matters is not the cost per click alone, but the <strong>cost per lead or sale</strong> and whether it fits your margins.</p>
        <p>Work backward with simple math. If one in ten visitors becomes a lead and one in five leads becomes a customer worth $1,000, a click can be worth around $20. If clicks cost less than that, the channel can be profitable; if they cost more, you need a better offer, a better page, or a different keyword. Use your own numbers, not industry averages.</p>

        <h2 id="budget">How to set a PPC budget</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Decide what a customer is worth.</strong> Use average profit per customer, not revenue.</li>
          <li><strong>Estimate your conversion rates</strong> from click to lead and lead to customer. Use conservative guesses until real data arrives.</li>
          <li><strong>Set a test budget.</strong> Choose an amount you can spend for four to eight weeks without panic, enough to gather meaningful data.</li>
          <li><strong>Set a daily cap and a stop-loss.</strong> Decide in advance when you will pause or change course.</li>
          <li><strong>Scale only what works.</strong> Increase budget on campaigns that produce leads at an acceptable cost.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="setup">How to set up your first PPC campaign</h2>
        <h3>1. Set up conversion tracking first</h3>
        <p>Before you spend a dollar, track the actions that matter: form submissions, phone calls, bookings or purchases. Without tracking you will not know what is working, and automated bidding will not have the data it needs. Our post on <a href="the-real-cost-of-not-tracking-your-marketing-funnel.html">the real cost of not tracking your marketing funnel</a> shows what that mistake costs.</p>
        <h3>2. Choose high-intent keywords</h3>
        <p>Start with searches that show buying intent, such as "emergency plumber in Austin" rather than "plumbing". Use match types and negative keywords to keep out irrelevant searches such as "free", "jobs" or "DIY".</p>
        <h3>3. Write ads that match the search</h3>
        <p>Include the keyword's meaning in the headline, state a clear benefit, and add a call to action. Add assets such as sitelinks, call buttons and location details.</p>
        <h3>4. Build a focused landing page</h3>
        <p>Send clicks to a page that matches the ad's promise, loads quickly on mobile, and makes the next step obvious. Sending everyone to the homepage is a common and costly mistake.</p>
        <h3>5. Choose a bidding approach</h3>
        <p>Beginners often start with a strategy focused on conversions once tracking is in place. For how automation works, read our guide on <a href="google-ads-automated-bidding-dynamic-search-ads-demand-gen.html">Google Ads automated bidding</a>.</p>
        <h3>6. Review weekly</h3>
        <p>Check search terms, remove wasteful ones, test new ad variations, and compare cost per lead against your target.</p>

        <h2 id="metrics">PPC metrics that matter</h2>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Click-through rate (CTR)</td><td>How often people click after seeing your ad</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost per click (CPC)</td><td>What you pay for each visit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conversion rate</td><td>The share of visitors who take the action you want</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost per lead / acquisition</td><td>What it costs to win a lead or customer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Return on ad spend (ROAS)</td><td>Revenue generated for each dollar spent</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>Rank decisions by cost per lead and return on ad spend. Clicks and impressions are useful diagnostics but rarely pay the bills.</p>

        <h2 id="google-ad-grants">Google Ad Grants for nonprofits</h2>
        <p>If you run a US nonprofit, <strong>Google Ad Grants</strong> can provide free search advertising. Program descriptions say eligible nonprofits receive up to $10,000 worth of clicks per month. US organizations generally need current 501(c)(3) status, and governmental organizations, hospitals and schools are typically excluded, though related fundraising arms can qualify.</p>
        <p>Accounts must follow program rules, which have included maintaining a 5% click-through rate, avoiding overly generic or single-word keywords, keeping keyword Quality Scores at a minimum level, setting up conversion tracking and completing an annual survey. Many grant accounts spend only a small share of the available credit because they struggle to meet these rules. Rules change, so confirm the current requirements with Google before applying.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common PPC mistakes to avoid</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Launching without conversion tracking.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Using broad keywords with no negatives,</strong> which pulls in irrelevant clicks.</li>
          <li><strong>Sending traffic to the homepage</strong> instead of a matching landing page.</li>
          <li><strong>Changing too much too quickly,</strong> which makes results impossible to read.</li>
          <li><strong>Optimizing for clicks</strong> instead of leads and sales.</li>
          <li><strong>Never reviewing search terms,</strong> allowing wasted spend to compound.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="ppc-vs-seo">PPC vs. SEO: which should you choose?</h2>
        <p>PPC buys visibility now and stops when the budget stops. SEO takes months to build but keeps working. Most growing businesses use both: PPC to generate leads quickly and test which keywords convert, then SEO to build lasting visibility around the terms that prove valuable. If you want to see how a strong SEO package is built, read our guide to <a href="seo-optimization-services-guide.html">SEO optimization services</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap manages PPC</h2>
        <p>We begin with tracking so every lead can be tied back to its source. Then we build tightly themed campaigns, write ads and landing pages that match intent, and review search terms and costs every week. Our goal is lower cost per lead, not more clicks. If you would like a second opinion on your account or a plan for your first campaign, book a strategy call.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What is pay per click advertising?</summary><p class="faq-a">Pay per click (PPC) advertising is a model where you pay a platform each time someone clicks your ad. On Google Ads, your ad enters an auction when someone searches, and you are charged only when a person clicks it.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How does Google Ads decide which ad shows first?</summary><p class="faq-a">Google uses Ad Rank, a set of values that decides whether your ad is eligible and where it appears. Google says Ad Rank takes into account your bid, the quality of your ad and landing page, minimum thresholds, the competitiveness of the auction, the search context, and the expected impact of ad assets and formats.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How much does pay per click advertising cost?</summary><p class="faq-a">It varies widely by industry, keyword, location and competition. You set a maximum bid and a daily budget, and your actual cost per click is often lower than your maximum. Higher quality ads can often lead to lower costs per click, according to Google.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is a good budget to start Google Ads as a small business?</summary><p class="faq-a">Start with an amount you can afford to spend for several weeks without expecting profit, enough to gather real conversion data. Many small businesses begin with a modest daily budget on one focused campaign, then scale what works.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What are Google Ad Grants?</summary><p class="faq-a">Google Ad Grants is a program that gives eligible nonprofits free Google Search advertising credit, up to $10,000 per month according to program descriptions. US organizations generally need 501(c)(3) status and must meet program rules, which have included a 5% click-through rate and keyword quality requirements. Check Google's current rules before applying.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is PPC better than SEO?</summary><p class="faq-a">They do different jobs. PPC delivers visibility immediately and stops when you stop paying. SEO takes longer but builds lasting visibility. Many businesses use PPC for fast results while SEO grows.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Ad Rank factors summarized from <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/1722122" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads Help: About Ad Rank</a>. Google Ad Grants details reflect third-party program summaries and change often; confirm current eligibility with Google. Tycoon Clap is not affiliated with Google.</p>
